# 50 hard English practice passages (evergreen topics), written for Alert Typing. Set 2.
EN = [
("Monetary Policy Transmission", "Monetary policy works only when changes in the policy rate actually reach households and businesses. When the Reserve Bank of India raises or lowers the repo rate, it expects banks to adjust the interest rates they charge on loans and pay on deposits. In practice, this transmission has often been slow and incomplete. Banks raise most of their funds through fixed-rate term deposits, so they hesitate to cut lending rates until those deposits are renewed at lower rates. Earlier systems of internal benchmarks, such as the base rate and the marginal cost of funds based lending rate, gave banks considerable discretion and therefore weakened the link. To improve matters, the Reserve Bank directed banks to link new floating-rate loans to retail customers and small enterprises to an external benchmark, usually the repo rate itself. Transmission has since become faster for new loans, though deposit rates still adjust gradually. Economists also point out that the health of bank balance sheets, the level of government borrowing and the liquidity conditions in the money market all influence how quickly a policy decision is felt across the wider economy."),
("The Anti-Defection Law", "The Tenth Schedule of the Constitution, added by the Fifty-second Amendment in 1985, was meant to curb the practice of legislators switching parties for office or money, which had repeatedly toppled elected governments. Under this law, a member of a House is disqualified if he voluntarily gives up the membership of his party or votes against its direction without permission. An independent member who joins a party after election, and a nominated member who joins a party after six months, also face disqualification. The original provision protected a split by one-third of a legislature party, but this exception was removed in 2003, leaving only a merger supported by two-thirds of the members. The power to decide disqualification petitions rests with the Speaker or Chairman, whose decision is subject to judicial review. Critics argue that the law has reduced the freedom of members to speak their conscience, while delays by presiding officers in deciding petitions have sometimes defeated its very purpose. Several committees have recommended that such petitions be decided by the President or Governor on the advice of the Election Commission."),
("Public-Private Partnerships in Infrastructure", "Public-private partnerships, commonly called PPPs, allow the government to build and operate infrastructure with private capital and management skills while retaining ownership and regulatory control. Under the build-operate-transfer model, a private concessionaire constructs a highway, port or airport, recovers its investment through tolls or user charges for a fixed period, and then hands the asset back to the government. The hybrid annuity model, introduced for national highways, reduces the risk for developers by having the government pay forty per cent of the project cost during construction and the remainder as annuities linked to performance. PPPs promise faster completion and better maintenance, but their record has been mixed. Aggressive bidding, delays in land acquisition, disputes over environmental clearances and unrealistic traffic projections have led many projects into financial distress. The Kelkar Committee recommended that risks be allocated to the party best able to manage them, that contracts be renegotiated through a transparent process, and that an independent regulator settle disputes quickly. Well-designed partnerships can still mobilise large investment at a time when public finances face many competing demands."),
("Mangroves and Coastal Protection", "Mangroves are salt-tolerant trees and shrubs that grow in the tidal zone between land and sea, mostly in tropical and subtropical regions. Their tangled roots trap sediment, slow down waves and protect coastlines from erosion, storm surges and cyclones. After the Indian Ocean tsunami of 2004, villages sheltered by dense mangrove belts in Tamil Nadu reported far less damage than those on open beaches. Mangrove forests are also nurseries for fish, crabs and prawns, supporting the livelihoods of lakhs of coastal families, and they store large amounts of carbon in their waterlogged soils. India's mangrove cover is concentrated in the Sundarbans of West Bengal, the Andaman and Nicobar Islands, the Gulf of Kachchh and the deltas of the Mahanadi, Godavari and Krishna. These forests face threats from conversion to aquaculture ponds, port construction, pollution and the diversion of fresh water upstream. Restoration programmes now involve local communities in planting saplings, regulating fishing and earning income from eco-tourism. Protecting mangroves is increasingly seen as a cost-effective alternative to concrete sea walls."),
("Separation of Powers", "The doctrine of separation of powers holds that the legislative, executive and judicial functions of government should be exercised by different organs, so that no single authority becomes all-powerful. Montesquieu argued that liberty cannot exist when the power to make laws, to execute them and to judge disputes is concentrated in the same hands. The Indian Constitution does not adopt this doctrine in its rigid American form. The executive is drawn from and remains responsible to the legislature, the President exercises legislative power through ordinances, and courts frame rules for their own procedure. Nevertheless, the Supreme Court has recognised a functional separation as part of the basic structure. Parliament cannot pass a law that simply overturns a judgment without removing its legal basis, and the executive cannot interfere with the appointment or transfer of judges at its pleasure. At the same time, courts are expected to show restraint in matters of policy, which are best left to elected representatives. The system thus relies on checks and balances, with each organ respecting the limits set for it by the Constitution."),
("Microfinance and Rural Credit", "Access to affordable credit has long been a problem for small farmers, landless labourers and women in rural India, who often depend on moneylenders charging exorbitant rates of interest. Microfinance seeks to fill this gap by offering small loans without collateral, usually to groups whose members guarantee each other's repayment. In India, two broad models have developed. The self-help group bank linkage programme, promoted by NABARD, connects groups of ten to twenty women with banks after they have saved regularly and maintained proper records. Microfinance institutions, on the other hand, lend directly to joint liability groups and collect weekly or monthly instalments. The sector grew rapidly but faced a crisis in Andhra Pradesh in 2010, when allegations of coercive recovery and multiple lending led to a state law restricting their operations. The Reserve Bank subsequently introduced regulations on interest margins, household income limits and fair practices. Studies show that microcredit helps families smooth consumption and start small enterprises, but lasting change requires financial literacy, access to markets and opportunities beyond subsistence activities."),
("Antimicrobial Resistance", "Antimicrobial resistance arises when bacteria, viruses, fungi and parasites change over time and no longer respond to the medicines that once killed them. Infections then become harder to treat, illness lasts longer and the risk of death increases. Every use of an antibiotic gives resistant organisms a chance to survive and multiply, but misuse greatly speeds up the process. In India, antibiotics are frequently bought without prescription, taken for viral fevers against which they are useless, or stopped before the full course is completed. Their widespread use in poultry and fish farming to promote growth adds to the problem, and effluents from pharmaceutical factories and hospitals carry resistant microbes into rivers. The World Health Organization has described antimicrobial resistance as one of the greatest threats to global health. India's National Action Plan calls for better surveillance, rational prescription, infection control in hospitals and research into new drugs and vaccines. The Red Line campaign marks certain antibiotic packs to warn people against using them without a doctor's advice. Public awareness remains the first line of defence."),
("The Office of the Governor", "The Governor is the constitutional head of a State, appointed by the President and holding office during his pleasure. Ordinarily, the Governor acts on the aid and advice of the Council of Ministers headed by the Chief Minister, but the Constitution also gives him certain discretionary powers. He may reserve a Bill passed by the State legislature for the consideration of the President, recommend President's Rule when the constitutional machinery breaks down, and invite a leader to form the government when no party has a clear majority. Disputes over the exercise of these powers have been frequent. The Sarkaria Commission and later the Punchhi Commission recommended that Governors be eminent persons detached from active politics, that they be consulted with the Chief Minister before appointment, and that a floor test in the Assembly, rather than the Governor's personal judgment, should decide who enjoys majority support. In the S. R. Bommai case, the Supreme Court held that a proclamation under Article 356 is open to judicial review. The office is meant to be a bridge between the Union and the States, not an agent of either."),
("Glacial Lakes and Flood Risk", "As Himalayan glaciers retreat because of rising temperatures, the meltwater collects behind loose walls of rock and ice, forming glacial lakes. Many of these lakes are growing in size and number, and some are dangerously unstable. A landslide, avalanche or earthquake can breach the natural dam, releasing millions of cubic metres of water within minutes. Such a glacial lake outburst flood carries boulders, mud and debris down narrow valleys, destroying bridges, roads, hydropower projects and villages. The disaster in Sikkim in October 2023, when the South Lhonak lake burst and swept away the Chungthang dam on the Teesta, showed how quickly the danger can turn into tragedy. The National Disaster Management Authority has identified several hundred high-risk lakes and has begun installing automatic weather stations, water level sensors and early warning systems. Controlled lowering of lake levels through siphoning or channel cutting, careful siting of infrastructure, and evacuation drills in vulnerable communities are other measures under consideration. Scientists stress that mapping and monitoring must continue throughout the year, not only during the monsoon."),
("Public Interest Litigation", "Public interest litigation emerged in India in the late nineteen-seventies, when judges such as P. N. Bhagwati and V. R. Krishna Iyer relaxed the traditional rule that only a person whose rights have been violated may approach a court. Any public-spirited citizen or organisation can now file a petition on behalf of those who are too poor, illiterate or powerless to seek justice themselves. The Supreme Court even treated letters and newspaper reports as writ petitions. Through such litigation, bonded labourers were released, undertrials languishing in jail were granted bail, and directions were issued on matters ranging from vehicular pollution in Delhi to sexual harassment at the workplace. The court also developed new remedies, appointing commissions to collect facts and continuing to monitor compliance for years. Over time, however, concerns have grown about frivolous petitions filed for publicity, private gain or political motives, which consume scarce judicial time. Courts have therefore begun imposing costs on such petitioners and insisting on proof of good faith. Used responsibly, public interest litigation remains a powerful instrument for making constitutional rights real for the marginalised."),
("Logistics Costs and Economic Growth", "Logistics covers the movement and storage of goods, from raw materials to finished products, across roads, railways, ports, warehouses and airports. In India, logistics costs have long been estimated at a high share of national output compared with developed economies, making Indian goods less competitive in world markets. A large share of freight travels by road, which is costlier and more polluting than rail or water transport. Delays at checkpoints, multiple documents, poor last-mile connectivity and fragmented warehousing add to the burden. The introduction of the Goods and Services Tax removed many interstate check posts and reduced the time trucks spend waiting at borders. The National Logistics Policy of 2022 and the PM Gati Shakti National Master Plan seek to integrate infrastructure planning across ministries using a common digital platform, so that new roads, rail lines and ports are designed to work together. Dedicated freight corridors are expected to shift bulk cargo to rail, while coastal shipping and inland waterways are being revived. Lower logistics costs would benefit farmers, small manufacturers and exporters alike."),
("Nutrition and Child Stunting", "Stunting, or low height for age, is a sign of chronic undernutrition during the first thousand days of a child's life, from conception to the second birthday. A stunted child is more likely to fall ill, perform poorly in school and earn less as an adult, so the effects last a lifetime and pass on to the next generation. Surveys in India have shown a slow decline in stunting, but the levels remain high, especially among poor, rural and tribal households. The causes go beyond the lack of food. Poor maternal health, early marriage, short gaps between pregnancies, low birth weight, inadequate breastfeeding, frequent diarrhoea due to unsafe water and open defecation all contribute. The Integrated Child Development Services scheme provides supplementary nutrition, immunisation and health check-ups through anganwadi centres, while the POSHAN Abhiyaan aims to improve coordination among departments and track growth through technology. Experts emphasise the role of women's education, fortified foods, a diverse diet including pulses, eggs and vegetables, and behaviour change campaigns. Nutrition must be treated as a shared responsibility of health, sanitation, agriculture and education."),
("Emergency Provisions of the Constitution", "Part Eighteen of the Constitution contains provisions for three kinds of emergencies. Under Article 352, the President may proclaim a national emergency when the security of India is threatened by war, external aggression or armed rebellion. During such an emergency, the Union can give directions to the States on any matter, Parliament can make laws on subjects in the State List, and the enforcement of certain fundamental rights may be suspended. Article 356 deals with the failure of constitutional machinery in a State, allowing the President to assume the functions of the State government. Article 360 permits a financial emergency when the financial stability or credit of India is threatened, though it has never been used. The experience of the internal emergency of 1975 to 1977 led to the Forty-fourth Amendment, which replaced the words internal disturbance with armed rebellion, required the written advice of the Cabinet, and provided that the rights under Articles 20 and 21 cannot be suspended. Parliamentary approval within one month and periodic renewal are further safeguards against misuse."),
("Semiconductor Manufacturing", "Semiconductors, or chips, are the brains of modern electronic devices, from mobile phones and computers to cars, medical equipment and defence systems. The global supply chain for chips is highly concentrated: a few companies design the most advanced chips, a handful of foundries mainly in East Asia fabricate them, and specialised firms supply the machines and materials used in production. The shortage of chips after the pandemic disrupted automobile and electronics industries worldwide and showed how vulnerable countries are to such concentration. India, which imports nearly all its semiconductors, launched the India Semiconductor Mission to attract investment in fabrication plants, display units and assembly, testing and packaging facilities, offering fiscal support for a large share of project costs. Several projects have been approved in Gujarat and Assam. Building a fabrication plant, however, requires enormous capital, uninterrupted power, ultra-pure water and a highly skilled workforce. India's strength in chip design, where thousands of engineers already work for global companies, offers a foundation on which a complete ecosystem may gradually be built."),
("Tribunals and Judicial Delays", "Tribunals were created to provide specialised and speedy adjudication in areas such as service matters, taxation, company law, consumer disputes and the environment, relieving the regular courts of a part of their heavy workload. Articles 323A and 323B, inserted by the Forty-second Amendment, enable Parliament and State legislatures to establish such bodies. Over time, however, tribunals have faced many of the same problems they were meant to solve. Vacancies remain unfilled for long periods, members are often retired officers with short tenures, and the parent ministries control their budgets and infrastructure, raising doubts about their independence. In a series of judgments, the Supreme Court has insisted that tribunals must enjoy the same independence as the courts they replace, that judicial members must have adequate experience, and that appeals should not bypass the High Courts. The Tribunals Reforms Act of 2021 abolished several tribunals and transferred their work back to the courts. Experts suggest a National Tribunals Commission to oversee appointments, administration and performance, so that these bodies deliver the quick and expert justice for which they were created."),
("Wetlands and the Ramsar Convention", "Wetlands are areas where water covers the soil or is present near the surface for much of the year, including marshes, swamps, lakes, floodplains, mangroves and paddy fields. They purify water, recharge groundwater, absorb floods, store carbon and provide habitat for fish, birds and countless other species. The Ramsar Convention, signed in the Iranian city of Ramsar in 1971, is an international treaty for the conservation and wise use of wetlands. Countries that join the convention designate sites of international importance and commit to maintaining their ecological character. India has steadily added to its list of Ramsar sites, which now includes Chilika lake in Odisha, Keoladeo National Park in Rajasthan, Loktak lake in Manipur and many others. Despite this recognition, wetlands across the country are shrinking because of urban expansion, encroachment, dumping of waste, sewage, siltation and invasive weeds. The Wetlands Conservation and Management Rules of 2017 require States to identify and notify wetlands and prohibit harmful activities within them. Involving local fishing and farming communities is essential for lasting conservation."),
("Urban Flooding", "Urban flooding has become a recurring problem in Indian cities, where a few hours of heavy rain can bring traffic to a standstill, inundate homes and disrupt essential services. Unlike river floods, these floods are largely the result of human decisions. Rapid construction has covered the ground with concrete, so rainwater cannot soak into the soil and runs off quickly. Lakes, ponds and natural drainage channels that once absorbed excess water have been encroached upon or filled in, while storm drains are clogged with plastic and silt. Many drainage systems were designed decades ago for much lower intensities of rainfall, which are becoming more frequent with climate change. The floods in Mumbai in 2005 and Chennai in 2015 exposed these weaknesses. Experts recommend mapping flood-prone areas, restoring lakes and wetlands, keeping drains free of encroachment, building rainwater harvesting structures and permeable pavements, and issuing ward-level rainfall warnings. The concept of sponge cities, which treats rainwater as a resource to be absorbed and stored rather than a nuisance to be drained away, is attracting interest among urban planners."),
("Fiscal Deficit and Public Debt", "The fiscal deficit is the difference between the total expenditure of the government and its total receipts excluding borrowings. It indicates how much the government must borrow in a year to meet its obligations. A moderate deficit can support growth, especially when borrowed money is spent on infrastructure and other productive assets, but persistent large deficits have serious consequences. They increase public debt, so that a growing share of revenue goes towards interest payments, leaving less for health, education and investment. Heavy government borrowing can also push up interest rates and crowd out private investment, while excessive monetisation may fuel inflation. The Fiscal Responsibility and Budget Management Act of 2003 set targets for reducing deficits, though these were relaxed during economic shocks such as the global financial crisis and the pandemic. The N. K. Singh Committee recommended using the ratio of debt to GDP as the main anchor of fiscal policy. Improving tax collection, rationalising subsidies and raising the quality of expenditure are considered the most sustainable ways of restoring fiscal health."),
("Telemedicine", "Telemedicine is the delivery of health care at a distance using information and communication technology, allowing doctors to consult patients through video calls, telephone or messaging, review reports online and monitor chronic conditions remotely. For a country where most specialists practise in cities while a large part of the population lives in villages, telemedicine offers a way to bridge the gap. The eSanjeevani platform, launched by the Union Health Ministry, has recorded a very large number of consultations, linking health and wellness centres in rural areas with doctors at district hospitals and medical colleges. The Telemedicine Practice Guidelines of 2020 gave legal clarity to registered medical practitioners on how to conduct such consultations and which medicines may be prescribed. Telemedicine reduces travel costs and waiting time, and proved especially valuable during the pandemic. Its limitations must also be recognised. A physical examination cannot always be replaced, poor internet connectivity hampers video calls, and elderly or less literate patients may find digital tools difficult. Protecting the privacy of patient records is equally important."),
("Directive Principles of State Policy", "The Directive Principles of State Policy, contained in Part Four of the Constitution, lay down the social and economic goals that the State should strive to achieve in making laws and policies. Inspired by the Irish Constitution, they direct the State to secure adequate means of livelihood for all citizens, equal pay for equal work, free legal aid, the organisation of village panchayats, a living wage for workers, free and compulsory education for children, the protection of the environment and the separation of the judiciary from the executive. Unlike fundamental rights, these principles are not enforceable in any court, yet Article 37 declares them fundamental in the governance of the country. In early cases, courts held that fundamental rights would prevail in case of conflict. In the Minerva Mills case, however, the Supreme Court emphasised that the Constitution rests on a balance between the two, and later judgments have read several directive principles into the right to life under Article 21. Many welfare laws, from land reforms to the right to education, owe their origin to these principles."),
("The Cooperative Movement", "A cooperative is an autonomous association of persons who unite voluntarily to meet their common economic and social needs through a jointly owned and democratically controlled enterprise. In India, the cooperative movement began with the Cooperative Credit Societies Act of 1904, aimed at freeing farmers from moneylenders. It later spread to dairy, sugar, fertiliser, housing, handloom and consumer sectors. The success of Amul, which grew from a small union of milk producers in Kheda district into a national brand, demonstrated how cooperatives can secure fair prices for small producers. Many cooperatives, however, have suffered from political interference, poor management, delayed elections and weak financial discipline. The Ninety-seventh Amendment of 2011 gave constitutional status to cooperative societies, though the Supreme Court later held that parts relating to State cooperatives required ratification by the States. The creation of a separate Ministry of Cooperation in 2021 and the computerisation of primary agricultural credit societies are recent efforts to strengthen the sector. Professional management, member participation and transparency remain the keys to a revived movement."),
("Satellite Navigation and NavIC", "Satellite navigation systems use signals transmitted by a constellation of satellites to determine the precise location and time of a receiver anywhere within their coverage. The American Global Positioning System is the most widely used, but several countries have developed their own systems to avoid dependence on foreign networks, particularly for defence. India's regional system, earlier called the Indian Regional Navigation Satellite System and now known as NavIC, consists of satellites in geostationary and geosynchronous orbits covering India and the region extending about fifteen hundred kilometres beyond its borders. It provides a standard positioning service for civilian users and an encrypted restricted service for authorised users. NavIC is used in vehicle tracking, fleet management, disaster warning, fishing boats and the mapping of land records. The government has encouraged mobile phone makers to support NavIC signals in their chipsets. Some satellites in the constellation have faced failures of their atomic clocks, and new satellites with indigenous clocks are being launched as replacements. A reliable domestic system is considered an important element of strategic autonomy."),
("Plea Bargaining", "Plea bargaining is a process in which an accused person agrees to plead guilty in return for a lesser sentence, avoiding a lengthy trial. It was introduced into Indian criminal law in 2006 through Chapter XXI-A of the Code of Criminal Procedure, following the recommendations of the Law Commission and the Malimath Committee, and the facility has been retained in the new criminal procedure law. It applies only to offences punishable with imprisonment of up to seven years, and is not available for offences affecting the socio-economic condition of the country or those committed against women or children below fourteen years of age. The application must be made voluntarily, and the court examines the accused in camera to ensure that there is no coercion. A mutually satisfactory disposition is then worked out, which may include compensation to the victim, after which the court may impose half of the minimum punishment prescribed. The scheme was expected to reduce the huge backlog of criminal cases and the number of undertrial prisoners, but it has been used sparingly because of limited awareness and a fear of stigma."),
("Minimum Support Price", "The minimum support price is the price at which the government announces it will purchase certain crops from farmers, protecting them against a sharp fall in market prices after a good harvest. It is announced before each sowing season for twenty-two mandated crops on the recommendation of the Commission for Agricultural Costs and Prices, which considers the cost of production, demand and supply, price trends, and the terms of trade between agriculture and other sectors. Since 2018, the government has fixed the support price at a level of at least one and a half times the cost of production as calculated by the commission. In practice, procurement is concentrated mainly on wheat and paddy in a few States with well-developed procurement systems, so many farmers elsewhere sell below the announced price. Critics say that the policy has encouraged water-intensive crops in Punjab and Haryana, depleting groundwater and reducing crop diversity. Supporters argue that it provides income security and builds the stocks needed for food security. Reforms under discussion include price deficiency payments and greater procurement of pulses and millets."),
("Desertification and Land Degradation", "Desertification is the degradation of land in arid, semi-arid and dry sub-humid areas caused by climatic variations and human activities. It does not mean the spread of existing deserts so much as the gradual loss of soil fertility, vegetation and water-holding capacity. In India, a significant part of the total geographical area is affected by some form of land degradation, through water erosion, wind erosion, salinity, waterlogging and the loss of vegetation cover. Overgrazing, deforestation, excessive use of chemical fertilisers, unscientific irrigation and mining are among the main causes. Degraded land produces less food and fodder, reduces rural incomes and forces people to migrate. India is a party to the United Nations Convention to Combat Desertification and has pledged to restore millions of hectares of degraded land by 2030. Watershed development, contour bunding, afforestation with native species, agroforestry, controlled grazing and the revival of traditional water bodies have shown good results where communities are fully involved. Schemes for soil health cards and micro-irrigation also help farmers use resources more efficiently and protect the land for future generations."),
("The Lokpal and Lokayuktas", "The idea of an independent ombudsman to investigate complaints of corruption against public servants was first recommended by the Administrative Reforms Commission in 1966, but legislation was delayed for decades. The Lokpal and Lokayuktas Act was finally passed in 2013 after a nationwide anti-corruption movement. The Lokpal consists of a chairperson and up to eight members, half of whom must be judicial members, and at least half must belong to the Scheduled Castes, Scheduled Tribes, Other Backward Classes, minorities or women. Its jurisdiction covers the Prime Minister, with certain safeguards, Union ministers, members of Parliament and officers of the Central Government. The selection committee includes the Prime Minister, the Speaker, the Leader of the Opposition, the Chief Justice of India and an eminent jurist. Every State was required to establish a Lokayukta within a year. The first Lokpal was appointed only in 2019. Observers note that the institution's effectiveness depends on adequate staff, its own investigating and prosecution wings, protection for whistle-blowers and a culture of swift action on complaints."),
("Heatwaves and Public Health", "A heatwave is a period of abnormally high temperatures, often accompanied by high humidity, that poses a serious risk to human health. The India Meteorological Department declares a heatwave in the plains when the maximum temperature crosses forty degrees Celsius and departs significantly from the normal. Heatwaves are becoming more frequent, intense and prolonged because of climate change, and urban areas suffer additionally from the heat island effect created by concrete, asphalt and the lack of green cover. Outdoor workers, the elderly, young children, pregnant women and people with chronic illnesses are most vulnerable to heat exhaustion and heat stroke, which can be fatal if not treated promptly. Ahmedabad was the first Indian city to prepare a heat action plan after a deadly heatwave in 2010, and many cities and States have since followed. Such plans include early warnings, the opening of cooling centres, the provision of drinking water at public places, changes in working hours for labourers and training for health workers. Cool roofs, more trees and better building design can reduce indoor temperatures over the long term."),
("Digital Public Infrastructure", "Digital public infrastructure refers to shared digital systems, built on open standards, that enable public and private services to be delivered at population scale. India's model is often described as a stack. Aadhaar provides a unique digital identity to more than a billion residents, the Unified Payments Interface allows instant transfers between bank accounts through mobile phones, and DigiLocker lets citizens store and share verified documents such as mark sheets and driving licences. The Account Aggregator framework enables individuals to share their financial data with lenders securely and with consent. Together these systems have lowered the cost of opening bank accounts, made direct benefit transfers possible, and encouraged a large number of small merchants to accept digital payments. Other countries have shown interest in adapting this approach, and India promoted it during its presidency of the G20. Challenges remain, including exclusion of people without smartphones or reliable connectivity, failures of biometric authentication, cyber fraud and the need for strong data protection. Designing systems that are inclusive, secure and interoperable is therefore as important as building them quickly."),
("Bad Loans and Banking Reform", "Non-performing assets are loans on which the borrower has stopped paying interest or principal for more than ninety days. During the last decade, Indian banks, especially public sector banks, accumulated a very high level of such bad loans, largely from corporate lending to infrastructure, steel and power projects during the boom years. An asset quality review ordered by the Reserve Bank in 2015 forced banks to recognise hidden stress, after which provisions for losses wiped out much of their profits. The government responded with a strategy of recognition, resolution, recapitalisation and reform. The Insolvency and Bankruptcy Code of 2016 created a time-bound process for resolving stressed companies, shifting control from defaulting promoters to creditors. The government injected fresh capital into public sector banks and merged several of them into larger entities to improve efficiency. Over the following years, the bad loan ratio declined substantially and bank profits recovered. Experts caution, however, that sound credit appraisal, independent boards, accountability for wilful defaulters and quick decisions by insolvency tribunals are needed to prevent a repeat of the crisis."),
("Soil Health and Organic Farming", "Healthy soil is a living system containing minerals, organic matter, water, air and billions of microorganisms that together support plant growth. Decades of intensive cultivation, monocropping and the unbalanced use of chemical fertilisers, particularly urea, have reduced the organic carbon content of many Indian soils and caused deficiencies of secondary and micronutrients such as sulphur, zinc and boron. The Soil Health Card scheme, launched in 2015, provides farmers with a report on the nutrient status of their fields and recommends the appropriate dose of fertilisers. Organic farming, which avoids synthetic chemicals and relies on compost, green manure, crop rotation and biological pest control, is promoted through the Paramparagat Krishi Vikas Yojana. Sikkim was declared the first fully organic State in the country. Supporters argue that organic methods improve soil structure, reduce input costs and fetch higher prices, while critics point to lower yields in the transition years and difficulties in certification. A balanced approach that combines organic manures with the judicious use of fertilisers, guided by soil testing, is widely recommended."),
("Inter-State River Water Disputes", "Rivers do not respect political boundaries, and disputes over the sharing of their waters have been a persistent source of tension among Indian States. Water is a State subject under the Constitution, but Article 262 allows Parliament to provide for the adjudication of disputes relating to inter-State rivers and to bar the jurisdiction of courts in such matters. The Inter-State River Water Disputes Act of 1956 provides for the constitution of tribunals, whose awards are final and binding once published by the Central Government. Long-running disputes include those over the Cauvery among Karnataka, Tamil Nadu, Kerala and Puducherry, the Krishna among Maharashtra, Karnataka, Telangana and Andhra Pradesh, and the Mahadayi between Goa and Karnataka. Tribunals have often taken many years to deliver their awards, and implementation has been delayed by appeals and political agitation. Proposed reforms include a single permanent tribunal with fixed time limits, a reliable national database of river flows and water use, and agencies to implement awards. Ultimately, efficient irrigation and cooperation in times of scarcity matter as much as legal settlements."),
("Child Labour and the Right to Education", "Child labour deprives children of their childhood, their health and their education, and traps families in a cycle of poverty. Despite laws and policies, a large number of children in India still work on farms, in homes as domestic helpers, in small workshops, brick kilns and roadside eateries. The Child and Adolescent Labour (Prohibition and Regulation) Act, as amended in 2016, prohibits the employment of children below fourteen years in all occupations, except in family enterprises after school hours, and bars adolescents between fourteen and eighteen from hazardous work. The Right of Children to Free and Compulsory Education Act of 2009 guarantees schooling to every child between six and fourteen years. Enforcement, however, remains weak, and poverty, migration, debt and the lack of good schools nearby push children into work. Rescue alone is not enough unless it is followed by rehabilitation, bridge courses to bring children back to school, and support to their families through employment schemes and social security. Community vigilance, responsible business practices and awareness among parents are equally necessary to end this injustice."),
("The Blue Economy", "The blue economy refers to the sustainable use of ocean resources for economic growth, better livelihoods and jobs while preserving the health of marine ecosystems. India has a coastline of more than seven thousand kilometres, a large exclusive economic zone and many islands, giving it considerable potential in fisheries, aquaculture, shipping, ports, coastal tourism, offshore energy and deep-sea minerals. The Sagarmala programme seeks to modernise ports and promote port-led industrialisation, while the Deep Ocean Mission aims to explore the ocean floor for minerals and develop technologies such as a manned submersible. Fisheries alone support the livelihoods of millions of people along the coasts. At the same time, overfishing, destructive trawling, plastic pollution, oil spills, coral bleaching and rising sea levels threaten the very resources on which the blue economy depends. Marine spatial planning, which allocates ocean areas for different uses based on scientific data, is being introduced to reduce conflicts. Cooperation among Indian Ocean countries on maritime security, search and rescue and scientific research is also an important part of this vision."),
("Fundamental Duties", "Fundamental duties were added to the Constitution as Article 51A by the Forty-second Amendment in 1976, on the recommendation of the Swaran Singh Committee, drawing inspiration from the constitution of the former Soviet Union. There are now eleven duties, the last of which, requiring parents and guardians to provide opportunities for education to their children between six and fourteen years, was added in 2002. Among other things, citizens are expected to abide by the Constitution and respect the national flag and anthem, cherish the noble ideals of the freedom struggle, uphold the sovereignty, unity and integrity of India, promote harmony and renounce practices derogatory to the dignity of women, protect the natural environment, develop a scientific temper and safeguard public property. These duties are not directly enforceable by courts, but the Supreme Court has used them while interpreting laws and judging the reasonableness of restrictions on rights. The Verma Committee identified several existing laws that give effect to these duties. Their real value lies in reminding citizens that rights and responsibilities go together in a democracy."),
("Data Protection and Privacy", "In the Puttaswamy judgment of 2017, a nine-judge Bench of the Supreme Court unanimously declared that the right to privacy is a fundamental right protected by Article 21 and other provisions of Part Three of the Constitution. The judgment emphasised that informational privacy, which concerns control over personal data, is an essential aspect of this right in a digital age. After several drafts and years of consultation, Parliament enacted the Digital Personal Data Protection Act in 2023. The law requires organisations that process personal data, called data fiduciaries, to obtain the free and informed consent of individuals, use the data only for the stated purpose, keep it secure and delete it when it is no longer needed. Individuals, called data principals, have the right to access information about their data, seek correction and erasure, and file grievances. A Data Protection Board adjudicates complaints and can impose heavy financial penalties for breaches. Critics have raised concerns about broad exemptions for the government and the independence of the Board, while businesses seek clarity on compliance. Building a culture of responsible data handling will take time."),
("Millets and Nutrition Security", "Millets are a group of small-seeded cereals such as jowar, bajra, ragi, kodo, kutki and foxtail millet, which were once a staple in large parts of India. They grow well on poor soils with little water, tolerate high temperatures and need few chemical inputs, making them suitable for dryland farming in a changing climate. Nutritionally, millets are rich in dietary fibre, protein, iron, calcium and other minerals, and have a lower glycaemic index than polished rice and wheat. After the Green Revolution, government support for rice and wheat through procurement and subsidised distribution led to a steep decline in millet cultivation and consumption. At India's initiative, the United Nations declared 2023 the International Year of Millets, and the government has since promoted them as nutri-cereals. Several States have included millets in the public distribution system, mid-day meals and anganwadi menus. Challenges include low yields, difficulties in processing and storage, limited market demand and short shelf life of flour. Investment in research, value-added products and assured procurement can help revive these climate-resilient grains."),
("Electric Vehicles and Battery Recycling", "Electric vehicles are seen as a key solution to urban air pollution and to reducing India's heavy dependence on imported crude oil. Two-wheelers and three-wheelers have led the transition, supported by central subsidies, lower goods and services tax, State incentives and lower running costs compared with petrol and diesel vehicles. Expanding the network of public charging stations, ensuring reliable electricity and bringing down the upfront cost of cars remain important challenges. The heart of an electric vehicle is its lithium-ion battery, which contains valuable materials such as lithium, cobalt, nickel and manganese. India has limited domestic reserves of these minerals and depends heavily on imports for battery cells. As the first generation of batteries reaches the end of its life, recycling becomes both an environmental necessity and an economic opportunity. The Battery Waste Management Rules of 2022 make producers responsible for collecting and recycling the batteries they sell and set targets for the recovery of materials. Second-life use of old batteries for storing solar power is another promising option before they are finally recycled."),
("Migrant Workers and Social Security", "Internal migration is a major feature of the Indian economy, with millions of workers moving from poorer States to cities and industrial regions in search of work in construction, manufacturing, transport, domestic service and hotels. Most of them work in the informal sector without written contracts, regular wages or social security. The sudden lockdown during the pandemic exposed their vulnerability, as countless workers lost their jobs and shelter overnight and began walking hundreds of kilometres to their villages. In its aftermath, the e-Shram portal was launched to create a national database of unorganised workers, and the One Nation One Ration Card scheme enabled migrants to draw subsidised food grains from any fair price shop in the country. The Code on Social Security of 2020 envisages schemes for gig and platform workers and unorganised labour. Experts stress that registration must be followed by real benefits such as health insurance, pensions, affordable rental housing in cities, portability of welfare entitlements, and access to schools for the children of migrant families. Migrants contribute greatly to growth and deserve dignity in return."),
("The Office of the Speaker", "The Speaker of the Lok Sabha is the presiding officer and the guardian of the rights and privileges of the House. Elected by the members from among themselves, the Speaker maintains order, interprets the rules of procedure, decides who may speak, and can suspend members for grave disorder. The Speaker decides whether a Bill is a Money Bill, and this certificate is final. Under the Tenth Schedule, the Speaker also decides petitions for the disqualification of members on the ground of defection. The Speaker presides over a joint sitting of the two Houses and does not vote in the first instance, but exercises a casting vote in the event of a tie. To ensure impartiality, the Speaker's salary is charged on the Consolidated Fund of India and his conduct in regulating the House cannot be questioned in any court. In Britain, the Speaker resigns from the party upon election and is usually re-elected unopposed, a convention not followed in India. Observers have often called for stronger conventions of neutrality, so that the opposition can rely on the chair to protect its voice."),
("Insurance Penetration in India", "Insurance protects individuals and businesses against financial losses arising from death, illness, accidents, natural disasters and damage to property. Insurance penetration, measured as premiums as a share of national income, remains low in India compared with the global average, and a large part of the population is underinsured or entirely uncovered. Many families still regard insurance mainly as an investment or tax-saving product rather than as protection, and claims disputes have damaged public trust. The government has launched several schemes to extend coverage, including the Pradhan Mantri Jeevan Jyoti Bima Yojana and Suraksha Bima Yojana, which offer life and accident cover at very low annual premiums through bank accounts, and the Ayushman Bharat scheme for hospital care. The limit on foreign direct investment in insurance companies has been raised in stages to attract capital and expertise. The Insurance Regulatory and Development Authority has set the goal of insurance for all by 2047. Simple products, transparent policy terms, quick settlement of claims, the use of technology for distribution and financial literacy are needed to close the protection gap."),
("Earthquake-Resistant Construction", "A large part of India's land area falls in seismic zones of moderate to very high risk, including the entire Himalayan belt, the north-east, parts of Gujarat and the Andaman and Nicobar Islands. Earthquakes themselves rarely kill people directly. It is the collapse of poorly built houses, schools and hospitals that causes most deaths and injuries. The Bhuj earthquake of 2001 showed how unreinforced masonry and badly designed multi-storey buildings can crumble within seconds. The Bureau of Indian Standards has issued codes for earthquake-resistant design and construction, which require features such as reinforced concrete bands at plinth, lintel and roof levels, vertical reinforcement at corners, proper anchoring of roofs and avoidance of soft storeys. The National Building Code also incorporates these provisions. Yet compliance is weak, especially in the rapid growth of informal construction in towns. Training masons, simplifying approval procedures, auditing the safety of existing public buildings and retrofitting vulnerable structures are urgently needed. Traditional building styles in places like Kashmir and Uttarakhand, using timber and stone in flexible combinations, also hold valuable lessons."),
("Judicial Review", "Judicial review is the power of the courts to examine the actions of the legislature and the executive and to declare them invalid if they violate the Constitution. In India, this power rests mainly on Articles 13, 32 and 226. Article 13 declares void any law that takes away or abridges fundamental rights, Article 32 allows citizens to approach the Supreme Court directly for the enforcement of these rights, and Article 226 gives High Courts a wide power to issue writs. The Supreme Court has held that judicial review is part of the basic structure, so even a constitutional amendment cannot take it away. Through this power, courts have struck down laws that were arbitrary or discriminatory, and have scrutinised executive actions for illegality, irrationality and procedural impropriety. At the same time, courts generally presume that laws are constitutional, defer to the legislature on economic policy, and avoid substituting their own judgment for that of experts. The proper balance between activism and restraint is constantly debated, but an independent judiciary with the power of review remains central to constitutional democracy."),
("Heritage Conservation and Tourism", "India's cultural heritage includes ancient temples, forts, stepwells, mosques, churches, cave paintings, historic towns and living traditions of music, dance, crafts and festivals. Many sites are protected by the Archaeological Survey of India under the Ancient Monuments and Archaeological Sites and Remains Act, while a growing number have been inscribed on the World Heritage List of UNESCO. Heritage attracts domestic and foreign tourists, generates employment for guides, artisans, hotel staff and transport operators, and contributes to local economies. Unregulated tourism, however, can damage the very assets it depends upon. Overcrowding, vandalism, encroachments, unplanned construction near monuments, pollution and the commercialisation of sacred spaces are common problems. Conservation requires trained specialists, traditional materials and careful documentation, and many lesser-known monuments receive little attention. Schemes such as Adopt a Heritage invite companies to develop amenities at sites, while heritage city development programmes aim to improve infrastructure around historic areas. Experts emphasise involving local communities, limiting visitor numbers at fragile sites and promoting responsible tourism that respects both monuments and the people living around them."),
("E-Waste Management", "Electronic waste includes discarded mobile phones, computers, televisions, refrigerators, washing machines, batteries and other electrical equipment. India is among the largest generators of electronic waste in the world, and the quantity is growing rapidly with rising incomes and shorter product lifespans. E-waste contains valuable materials such as gold, silver, copper and rare earth elements, but also hazardous substances like lead, mercury, cadmium and brominated flame retardants. Most of it is handled by the informal sector, where workers, including children, dismantle devices by hand, burn cables in the open and use acids to extract metals, exposing themselves and the surrounding environment to toxic pollution. The E-Waste Management Rules, revised in 2022, are based on the principle of extended producer responsibility, requiring manufacturers to ensure that a specified quantity of their products is collected and recycled through registered recyclers, with tradable certificates and an online portal. Effective implementation requires more authorised collection centres, formal recognition and training of informal workers, consumer awareness and product designs that are easier to repair, upgrade and recycle."),
("Universal Health Coverage", "Universal health coverage means that all people have access to the health services they need, of good quality, without suffering financial hardship. In India, out-of-pocket spending on medicines, tests and hospital care pushes many families into poverty every year, because public spending on health has remained low as a share of national income. The National Health Policy of 2017 set a goal of raising government health expenditure substantially. Ayushman Bharat has two components. Health and wellness centres, now called Ayushman Arogya Mandirs, aim to provide comprehensive primary care, including screening for diabetes, hypertension and common cancers, close to people's homes. The Pradhan Mantri Jan Arogya Yojana offers poor and vulnerable families health cover for secondary and tertiary hospitalisation in empanelled public and private hospitals. Challenges include shortages of doctors and nurses in rural areas, uneven quality of care, low awareness of entitlements, delayed payments to hospitals and fraud. Strengthening primary health care, expanding generic medicines and building digital health records are considered essential steps towards health for all."),
("The Model Code of Conduct", "The Model Code of Conduct is a set of guidelines issued by the Election Commission of India for political parties and candidates during elections. It comes into force from the date on which the election schedule is announced and remains in operation until the results are declared. The code prohibits ministers from announcing new schemes, projects or financial grants that may influence voters, from using official machinery, vehicles and government accommodation for campaigning, and from making transfers of officials connected with elections. It bars appeals to caste or communal feelings, the use of places of worship for propaganda, bribery and intimidation of voters, and criticism of private lives of opponents. The code began as a voluntary agreement among parties in Kerala in 1960 and has gradually gained acceptance across the country, although it does not have statutory backing. Violations are dealt with through warnings, censure, bans on campaigning for a period, and, where offences under criminal law are involved, the registration of cases. Its effectiveness depends on the Commission's impartiality and the willingness of parties to respect democratic fairness."),
("Crop Insurance", "Agriculture in India remains highly exposed to the vagaries of weather, pests and diseases, and a single bad season can wipe out a farmer's investment and push the family into debt. Crop insurance is meant to cushion farmers against such losses. The Pradhan Mantri Fasal Bima Yojana, launched in 2016, replaced earlier schemes and provides comprehensive cover from sowing to post-harvest losses at low premiums, with farmers paying two per cent of the sum insured for kharif crops, one and a half per cent for rabi crops and five per cent for commercial and horticultural crops, while the Centre and States share the rest. The use of satellite imagery, drones and smartphones for crop-cutting experiments has been introduced to speed up the assessment of yields. The scheme was made voluntary for all farmers in 2020. Complaints persist about delayed settlement of claims, disputes over yield data, low awareness and the withdrawal of some States citing high premium costs. Experts suggest weather-based index insurance, better use of technology, timely payment of the government's share of premiums and stronger grievance redressal."),
("Startups and the Innovation Ecosystem", "India has emerged as one of the largest startup ecosystems in the world, with tens of thousands of recognised startups in sectors such as financial technology, education, health, agriculture, logistics, retail and software services. Several of them have grown into unicorns, private companies valued at more than a billion dollars. The Startup India initiative, launched in 2016, offers self-certification under labour and environmental laws, faster patent examination, tax benefits for eligible firms and a fund of funds that invests through venture capital funds. Digital public infrastructure, a large pool of engineers and a growing domestic market have created fertile ground for innovation. Yet the ecosystem faces challenges. A large share of funding has gone to a few consumer internet businesses, many startups have struggled to become profitable, and funding slowed sharply in some years. Startups in smaller towns and those founded by women find it harder to raise capital. Experts call for more investment in deep technology and research, closer links between universities and industry, simpler regulations and patient capital to support long-term innovation."),
("Hydrogen as a Clean Fuel", "Hydrogen is the most abundant element in the universe, and when used as a fuel it produces only water, making it attractive for reducing carbon emissions. It can power fuel cell vehicles, replace natural gas in industrial heating and serve as a feedstock for fertilisers and refineries. Most hydrogen produced today, however, comes from natural gas or coal and is therefore called grey hydrogen, because its production releases large amounts of carbon dioxide. Green hydrogen is produced by splitting water through electrolysis using electricity from renewable sources such as solar and wind power. India launched the National Green Hydrogen Mission in 2023 with the aim of developing a large production capacity by 2030, supported by incentives for electrolyser manufacturing and hydrogen production. Sectors that are difficult to electrify, such as steel, fertilisers, shipping and long-distance transport, are expected to benefit the most. The main obstacles are the high cost of electrolysers and renewable power, the large amount of water required, and the difficulty of storing and transporting hydrogen safely. Falling costs of solar energy give India a competitive advantage."),
("Rights of Persons with Disabilities", "The Rights of Persons with Disabilities Act of 2016 replaced an earlier law of 1995 and brought Indian legislation in line with the United Nations Convention on the Rights of Persons with Disabilities. It expanded the recognised types of disability from seven to twenty-one, including acid attack victims, people with autism, thalassaemia, Parkinson's disease and specific learning disabilities. The Act guarantees equality and non-discrimination, and requires government establishments to reserve four per cent of vacancies for persons with benchmark disabilities and higher educational institutions to reserve five per cent of seats. Every child with a benchmark disability between six and eighteen years is entitled to free education. Public buildings, transport and websites are required to be made accessible within specified time limits, and the Accessible India Campaign was launched to achieve this. Discrimination against persons with disabilities is punishable under the Act. In practice, many buildings remain inaccessible, assistive devices are costly, and social attitudes often treat disability as a matter of charity rather than rights. Inclusive education, accessible workplaces and the active participation of disabled persons in policy-making are essential."),
]
